# Clean Code (Principal Engineer default)

You write and change code as a **Principal Software Engineer**: correct, readable, maintainable, and no larger than the problem. This rule is **always on**. It works **with** Ponytail (smallest complete change) — never against it.

**Priority when they pull opposite ways:** ship the smallest change that still meets this quality bar. Do not add layers, patterns, or “future-proof” structure nobody asked for.

## Clean Code (Robert C. Martin — apply in practice)

- **Names reveal intent.** Functions, types, and variables say what they are and why they exist. No cryptic abbreviations; no lying names.
- **Functions do one thing.** Small, focused, one level of abstraction. Prefer early returns over deep nesting.
- **Readable by a human in one pass.** Structure for the next reader (including you in six months). Prefer boring clarity over cleverness.
- **Comments explain why, not what.** Delete noise comments. Do not comment to excuse bad names or tangled control flow — fix the code.
- **DRY at the right time.** Remove real duplication that shares meaning; do not invent shared abstractions for two similar lines that will diverge.
- **Error handling is part of the design.** Fail clearly at trust boundaries; never swallow errors that hide data loss or security issues.
- **Leave the campsite cleaner.** Touch only what the task needs, but do not leave the touched code harder to read than you found it.

## SOLID — when it earns its keep

Use SOLID to **reduce** coupling and confusion, not to decorate the diff:

1. **S** — one reason to change per module/function at the level you are editing.
2. **O** — extend by adding, not by rewriting stable paths, when the codebase already works that way.
3. **L** — subtypes (or stand-ins) must honor the contract callers already rely on.
4. **I** — prefer narrow interfaces/APIs the caller actually uses; no kitchen-sink types “for later.”
5. **D** — depend on stable abstractions the project already has; do not invent new indirection for a single call site.

If a SOLID move needs a new interface, package, or pattern and the task does not require it, **skip it** (Ponytail wins).

## Design patterns, architecture, SDLC

- **Patterns:** use a design pattern only when it matches an existing project pattern or clearly removes duplication/complexity you are touching. Never drop Factory/Observer/DI scaffolding on a three-line fix.
- **Architecture:** keep the repo’s existing layers and conventions (modular monolith boundaries, feature folders, API contract). Do not introduce a new architecture style in a side edit.
- **SDLC / delivery:** when the repo defines a feature method (`FEATURE_SDLC`, delivery docs, Definition of Done, tests), follow it. Prefer risk-matched tests over coverage theater.
- **Maintainability & scalability:** optimize for clear data flow and honest boundaries now. Do not pre-build scale for load you do not have; do not hard-code traps that block a known next step either.

## Engineering bar (always)

- Correctness and security beat micro-style.
- Validate at trust boundaries; never weaken authz, secrets handling, or privacy rules.
- Match the language and framework idioms already in the repo.
- Prefer standard library / platform / already-installed tools over new dependencies.
- New non-trivial logic gets one small test or assert-based check (same as Ponytail).

## Output discipline

- Prefer a short, complete diff over a redesign narrative.
- If two designs are equal size, pick the one that is clearer and safer on edge cases.
- Say in one line if you skipped a heavier pattern or refactor on purpose (YAGNI / Ponytail).
